plan InspectionAndArtifacts
title   Inspection, portable artifacts, and evidence comparison
status  archived
goal
    Let consumers inspect committed behavior, retain bounded evidence, exchange canonical
    artifacts, and share explicit redacted projections without acquiring execution authority. This
    proposed Plan owns structural codecs, portable artifact schemas, inspection, redaction, and
    comparison.

outcomes
    O1  Pure inspection and committed live projections
        - Pure graph/transition functions consume inert definitions and snapshots. They may expose
        selection, microsteps, memory patches, and desired plans, never actual starts/releases,
        generations, pending outcomes, or finalizers.

        - Live inspection consumes committed private TurnRecord/LifecycleRecord facts. No
        arbitrary-snapshot trace or second mutable history exists. TurnRecord and v2 models stay
        private; projections are inferred and accepted only when validated or created by the
        inspection route.

        - One formatter belongs to each projection. Supported route names and known forms follow
        below. Unspecified signatures remain @Q2.

        - @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7 owns snapshot/current-state publication, including latest
        current snapshots for slow readers; evidence retention here does not imply replay
        streams. @V1 proves prediction/live separation, passive inspection, and private type
        boundaries.

        - The inspect route targets these exact names, subject to PackageContract route gates:

        | Family | Names |
        | --- | --- |
        | Graph and transitions | graphOf, inspectTransition |
        | Microsteps and activities | inspectMicrosteps, inspectActivities |
        | No-transition explanation | whyNoTransition, formatNoTransitionSummary |
        | Live trace projections | analyzeTrace, summarizeTrace, diffTrace, formatTrace |
        | Inspection formatting | formatInspectionEvent, formatInspectionTimeline |
        | Domain reports | formatRehydrationSummary, formatResourceFreshnessReport |
        | Transaction report | formatTransactionOverlapSummary |
        | Behavior artifacts | buildBehaviorContract, sliceBehaviorContract, diffBehaviorContracts |
        | Behavior rendering | renderBehaviorContract, renderBehaviorCoverage, renderBehaviorDiff |
        | Sinks | attachInspectionSink, createInspectionBufferSink |
        | Byte artifacts | importTraceArtifact, exportTraceArtifact |
        | Compression | compressTraceArtifact, decompressTraceArtifact |

        - Specified call forms preserve exact machine relationships. M denotes the supplied
        Machine; snapshot/event use that exact M. Projection returns remain inferred and
        package-owned, with no public Flow* or v2 model hierarchy. Forms left open at @Q2 are
        unapproved.

        | Function | Parameters | Return |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | graphOf | machine:M | Inferred graph for M |
        | inspectTransition | machine:M, snapshot of M, EventOf<M> | Transition for M |
        | inspectMicrosteps | machine:M, snapshot of M, EventOf<M> | Inferred microstep projection |
        | whyNoTransition | machine:M, snapshot of M, EventOf<M> | Explanation or undefined |
        | analyzeTrace | @Q2 | Unapproved; exact form unresolved |
        | summarizeTrace | trace | Inferred retained-window summary |
        | diffTrace | left, right | Inferred diff preserving both operands |
        | formatTrace | trace | string |
        | formatInspectionEvent | event | string |
        | formatInspectionTimeline | readonly event array | string |
        | formatNoTransitionSummary | explanation | string |
        | formatRehydrationSummary | trace | string |
        | formatResourceFreshnessReport | trace | string |
        | formatTransactionOverlapSummary | trace | string |
        | buildBehaviorContract | target | Inferred behavior contract; target gap at @Q2 |
        | sliceBehaviorContract | @Q2 | Unapproved; exact form unresolved |
        | diffBehaviorContracts | left, right, options? | Inferred behavior diff |
        | renderBehaviorContract | contract, options? | string |
        | renderBehaviorCoverage | target, options? | string; target gap at @Q2 |
        | renderBehaviorDiff | @Q2 | Unapproved; exact form unresolved |

        - `diffBehaviorContracts` and `renderBehaviorContract` options are exactly
        { moduleId?:string }. A missing option adds no alternate projection. `renderBehaviorDiff`
        has no approved form at @Q2. Pure graph/transition inspection starts no work and never
        claims actual starts/releases/generations/pending outcomes/finalizers. Live projections
        consume only the accepted committed evidence model. whyNoTransition returning undefined
        for no explanation is not a codec failure channel.

        - createInspectionBufferSink({ capacity? }) defaults to 256. Its exact buffer members are:

        ```ts
        snapshot(): { records: InspectionRecord[]; truncatedBeforeSequence: number | null };
        clear(): void;
        ```

        - InspectionRecord is private notation. attachInspectionSink(runtime,sink) returns
        { drain, dispose }; drain is awaitable. Each sink is route-created and attaches once.
        @O3 owns retention, failure, and accepted-prefix semantics.

        - importTraceArtifact(bytes:Uint8Array) returns an inferred private raw trace.
        exportTraceArtifact(trace,options?) returns Uint8Array under @O5. The byte carrier is
        defensive and contains no filesystem authority. No v1 object importer is retained.

        - compressTraceArtifact takes the inferred trace operand; decompressTraceArtifact takes
        Uint8Array. Their asynchronous failure/return form is unresolved at @Q2 because old
        undefined/v1 carriers conflict with the accepted v2 typed-failure boundary. No share
        input is accepted. Compression helpers imply no executable model or extra decoder.

    O2  Shared portable structural codec
        - Persistence restore and raw artifact import/export share one Flow-owned structural
        walker, private Effect Schema decoder, and package-owned canonical decoded data. Share
        output uses that structural encoding policy; it has no decoder.

        - Persistence owns its distinct envelope and opaque application codec under
        @plan.Persistence.O1 and @plan.Persistence.O2. Public bytes are strict UTF-8 stable-key
        JSON or exactly one gzip member. Stop decompression on crossing the decompressed bound.

        - Validate structure, versions, identity, descriptors, tokens, revisions, and bounds
        before actor creation, external work, or committed mutation. Exact limits, ordering,
        finite-number rules, compression rejection, failure ownership, and portable host laws
        follow below.

        - No raw parser/library exception, undefined failure, generic corruption result, Node
        Buffer, Cause tree, or envelope-specific limit override escapes. DiagnosticProjection
        remains the one diagnostic model under @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O1 and
        @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O2.

        - @V2 proves byte/structure bounds, hostile carriers, portable bytes, and pre-mutation
        failure.

        ### Exact structural bounds

        | Limit | Required value |
        | --- | --- |
        | Depth | 32, root at zero |
        | Visited nodes | 10,000 scalar-or-container nodes |
        | Array length | 4,096 items |
        | One string or property key | 262,144 UTF-8 bytes |
        | Decompressed or canonical encoded UTF-8 | 2,097,152 bytes |

        - Bounds apply uniformly to boot, behavior, trace, shares, and opaque carriers. No
        envelope overrides exist. Compressed input stops when the decompressed-byte limit is
        crossed. Counters, revisions, generations, sequences, and timestamps are non-negative
        safe integers.

        - A boot counter already at MAX_SAFE_INTEGER is invalid; reserve terminal-publication and
        Runtime-cleanup credits.

        - The wire name NonNegative means Number.isSafeInteger(value) && value >= 0. Public
        timestamps are integer epoch milliseconds from Runtime Effect Clock; Stories/evidence
        use TestClock.

        ### Portable bytes and canonical order

        - Use shared host-neutral ECMAScript/Web text APIs and WHATWG TextEncoder byte counts.
        Reject lone surrogates before encoding, using String.prototype.isWellFormed or an
        equivalent fallback.

        - Never silently replace surrogates, approximate UTF-8 with UTF-16 length, use Node Buffer
        in shared code, use host-specific encoders, or add a second handwritten encoder.

        - Node, Bun, Deno, and browser consumers receive equal canonical bytes and stable-ref
        identities from equal accepted input.

        - Canonical JSON sorts object keys, ID-indexed declarations, and requirement lists by
        UTF-8 byte order. Preserve authored child, event, checkpoint, record, diagnostic, and
        fact order. Finite numbers use JSON.stringify. Perform no Unicode normalization.

        - Add exactly one trailing newline at the file boundary. Artifacts are stable-key UTF-8
        JSON and are emitted uncompressed.

        - These artifact-order rules do not replace Resources canonical K record ordering or its
        -0 normalization. Stable descriptor/ref identity bounds belong to ApplicationsAndActors;
        the shared UTF-8 helper provides the bytes, without type-level byte arithmetic.

        ### Defensive validation and failure ownership

        - Public byte processing is strict UTF-8, at most one supported compression member,
        decompressed bounds, JSON parsing, and the one package-private Effect Schema decoder to
        fresh canonical data.

        - Validate versions, identities, descriptors, tokens, revisions, and limits before mutation
        or work. Synchronous Runtime construction remains inert and performs no storage read.

        - When ready executes, Persistence reads and validates detached boot data. Its application
        codec validates opaque values before service acquisition, store installation, activation,
        or handle escape. @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O3 and @plan.Persistence.O2 own that boundary.

        - Do not decode compiled machine input again or run boot-domain application decoding for
        artifact-only operations.

        - Reject malformed/cyclic data, serialized negative zero, non-finite numbers, parser
        failures, detectable duplicate object keys, concatenated gzip members, trailing bytes,
        unsupported compression, and over-limit input.

        - Require duplicate-key detection when the parser provides it. Compression and
        decompression failure map to DecompressionFailed or the applicable bounded-input
        diagnostic.

        - Private direct-decoder behavior is unspecified for proxies, getters, custom prototypes,
        descriptors, symbol properties, sparse arrays, and concurrent mutation; this does not
        weaken public byte rules.

        - Failures retain structural, bound, identity, decompression, external-input, application,
        cleanup, interruption, I/O, or invariant ownership. Bounds identify limit and path
        separately from other categories.

        - Diagnostic owns classification/code/path/details plus evolving summary/help. Ordered
        wire projections do not invent another type or mirror Cause.

        - Raw parser errors, generic corruption, undefined failures, executable authority, and raw
        Effect Cause never escape in carriers.

    O3  Ordered bounded inspection evidence
        - Every successful turn exposes one atomic committed actor/store cut. Accepted evidence
        uses one Runtime-global sequence shared by turn and lifecycle facts. Acknowledgment
        follows publication/evidence acceptance and never waits for user Effects. StoreFanout is
        causal.

        - A lifecycle snapshot precedes its event through the existing handle; lifecycle facts do
        not advance machine-turn revision or manufacture TurnRecords.

        - Lifecycle data includes exact actor/app/plan/incarnation provenance, full snapshot,
        from/to/cause, both private revisions, timestamp, and sequence. Fresh snapshot
        publication revision is zero; hydration restores persisted publication and turn
        revisions.

        - @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5 and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7 owns lifecycle
        validity and publication. No prepare record or terminal-disposal machine turn exists.
        createInspectionBufferSink({ capacity? }) defaults to 256 and requires a non-negative
        integer.

        - Caller capacity is exact, including zero. snapshot() returns records and
        truncatedBeforeSequence:number|null; clear():void preserves sequence. The marker is null
        iff no accepted evidence was omitted, otherwise the greatest omitted or cleared
        sequence.

        - Clear advances only through the retained tail; accepted queued records may arrive later.
        One sink attaches once to one Runtime and observes post-attachment accepted records
        only. Before attachment snapshot is empty/null.

        - Late attachment marks greatest unseen sequence, except sequence zero. drain/dispose wait
        only through accepted prefixes before detaching. Slow sinks never delay
        acknowledgment/StoreFanout, mutate/roll back commits, or retain actor-owned history.

        - Failure detaches that attachment while other sinks continue. No silent omission,
        mixed-Runtime state, persisted buffers, or second mutable history is permitted. Ordinary
        admission/commits reserve actor/store/sequence cleanup capacity before mutation.

        - Sequence exhaustion refuses work before commit. Disposal drains terminal lifecycle
        evidence in reverse dependency order. Changed-ref hints apply only to their revision;
        skipped readers reread relevant current state rather than reconstructing incomplete
        historical diffs.

        - Records become durable only by explicit configured-sink export, preserving order,
        marker, app identity, and format version. @V3 proves ordering, isolation, capacity, and
        drain races.

    O4  Canonical raw behavior and trace schemas
        - Available fields, discriminants, invariants, canonical fingerprint rules, and fixed
        condition-to-code mappings follow below. A behavior artifact has kind behavior-contract
        and version flow-state/behavior-contract.v2.

        - It carries appId, persistenceVersion, appPlanFingerprint, requirements, modules,
        machines, and app/machine Story metadata. It contains declarations/coverage identity
        only.

        - A raw trace has kind trace-artifact and version flow-state/trace-artifact.v2 with
        storyId, appId, persistenceVersion, appPlanFingerprint, capturedAt,
        truncatedBeforeSequence, ordered records, checkpoints, outcome, end, failure, and
        cleanup.

        - Nullable fields are present as null. Completed traces have end, null failure, and
        complete cleanup. Failed traces have failure and cleanup consistent with ordered
        diagnostics. End is never finality.

        - Resource/transaction/stream facts remain closed, with exact
        generation/occurrence/retention/ latest/reconciliation unions; no unknown fields,
        omitted nulls, legacy root/child/final/v1/ Scenario shapes, duplicate checkpoint names,
        invalid lifecycle tuple, unresolved requirement, duplicate identity, invalid Story
        metadata/default, or fingerprint mismatch is accepted.

        - No callbacks, fixtures, Effects, live actors/refs/Runtime state, raw Cause, or public v2
        model is serialized. Story and CLI share one private decoded handoff. @V4 precisely
        records source layouts that remain undeclared; it blocks claiming exact schema
        completeness.

        - @V4 proves explicit schema/canonical bytes/fingerprint requirements and closes only
        after the @V4 layouts and decisive executable schema vectors exist.

        ### Behavior artifact

        | Field | Required value or meaning |
        | --- | --- |
        | kind | "behavior-contract" |
        | version | "flow-state/behavior-contract.v2" |
        | appId | Exact stable app identity |
        | persistenceVersion | Exact application persistence version |
        | appPlanFingerprint | Canonical lowercase-hex SHA-256 identity |
        | requirements | Normalized one-record-per-static-identity requirement table |
        | modules | Exact module ownership/references |
        | machines | Exact declarations, compound defaults, operation/context identity |
        | Story metadata | App/machine metadata; private representation pending at @V4 |

        - A behavior artifact contains declaration and coverage identity only. Each operation
        requirement ID resolves to exactly one requirements record. No fixtures, Effects, live
        actor objects, Runtime state, or executable callbacks are stored.

        - Fingerprint preimage is canonical { appId, persistenceVersion, requirements, modules,
        machines }. Exclude Stories and trailing newline. The package-private behavior builder
        owns the preimage; encoding recomputes/verifies the emitted fingerprint.

        - Standalone behavior import recomputes the fingerprint from its included preimage and
        rejects inconsistent bytes as ArtifactIdentityMismatch. Standalone trace import validates
        its identity fields and fingerprint form; it has no receiving App or declaration preimage.

        - At an existing boundary supplied with a compiled receiving AppPlan, compare that App's
        fingerprint with the artifact. Mismatch is ArtifactIdentityMismatch. Artifact comparisons
        also reject incompatible operand identities. Standalone import requires no App or gateway.

        - Byte import gains no App argument, hidden global registry, or implicit gateway loading.
        No public fingerprint or collision-resolution API exists.

        ### Raw trace artifact

        ```ts
        type NonNegative = number; // runtime: safe integer >= 0
        type Nullable<A> = A | null;
        ```

        | Field | Required value or meaning |
        | --- | --- |
        | kind | "trace-artifact" |
        | version | "flow-state/trace-artifact.v2" |
        | storyId | Exact registered Story identity |
        | appId | Exact stable App identity |
        | persistenceVersion | Exact application persistence version |
        | appPlanFingerprint | Verified behavior identity |
        | capturedAt | NonNegative Clock timestamp |
        | truncatedBeforeSequence | Nullable<NonNegative>; greatest omitted sequence |
        | records | Ordered committed TurnRecord/LifecycleRecord projections |
        | checkpoints | Ordered uniquely named checkpoints |
        | outcome | "completed" or "failed" |
        | end | Checkpoint or null; never finality |
        | failure | StoryFailure or null |
        | cleanup | Complete, or failed with ordered diagnostics |

        - Completed outcome requires non-null end, null failure, and { status: "complete" }
        cleanup. Failed outcome requires failure, nullable end, and cleanup status agreeing with
        its diagnostics. Failure before end capture leaves end null. Failure after end capture
        retains end.

        - Cleanup failure cannot produce successful evidence. Checkpoint captures include exact
        actor targets plus reserved Runtime now/pendingWork metadata; machine evidence exposes
        its single snapshot directly.

        - All nullable fields are present and null when absent. Unknown members and omitted
        nullable members reject.

        - Preserve exact record/checkpoint/fact/diagnostic order, sequence, lifecycle snapshot,
        context and observed provider revisions, StoreState revision, timer identity, operation
        occurrence/generation, and issue ownership.

        - The decoder rejects a truncation marker inconsistent with retained records. A non-null
        marker prevents proof and complete equality.

        ### Record, operation, and failure constraints

        - Lifecycle evidence contains full lifecycle snapshot; exact actor/app/plan/incarnation
        provenance; from/to/cause; publication and machine-turn revisions; timestamp; and
        Runtime-global sequence. actor:restore is prepared -> prepared and precedes actor:start.
        actor:start is first activation; actor:suspend is active -> suspended; actor:resume is
        suspended -> active; actor:dispose is terminal from any non-disposed lifecycle.

        - Fresh creation, restore, attachment, owner disposal, and Runtime disposal are distinct
        causes. No prepare event, machine turn for lifecycle, or synthetic terminal TurnRecord
        is allowed. ApplicationsAndActors owns transition validity.

        | Fact | Closed status lanes and required constraints |
        | --- | --- |
        | Resource | missing, pending, ready, refreshing, failure, defect, interrupted |
        | Transaction | idle, pending, success, failure, defect, interrupted, unknown |
        | Stream | idle, running, complete, interrupted, failure, defect |

        - Transaction wire facts preserve Flow's attempt evidence under @plan.Transactions.O4.
        They do not capture the adapter's private remote identity or journal or grant recovery
        authority. Unknown transaction has reconcileRequired:true. Stream facts preserve exact
        hasValue/latest/ emissionCount correlation: latest is present exactly in the hasValue
        lane that permits it.

        - Missing/idle operation lanes have null generation/occurrence where specified;
        inconsistent generation, occurrence, retention, latest, or reconciliation rejects as
        InvalidTraceRecord before acquisition.

        - Canonical operation projection discriminants/absence/retention remain owned by
        Resources, Transactions, and Streams; copying them into wire facts never creates new
        operation behavior. Context, store, timer, and issue facts retain exact identities and
        authored order.

        - The precise JSON field sets/correlation for these facts, and the operation wire fields
        beyond explicit laws, remain pending Spec design at @V4 rather than inferred layouts.

        - Only ordered DiagnosticProjection values are serialized. Preserve independently
        actionable cleanup diagnostic order and multiplicity, with no Effect Cause shape,
        traversal order, or fiber ordinals.

        - Only the in-process FlowDisposeError and FlowStoryExecutionError retain complete Cause
        after classification/cleanup aggregation.

        - The Story envelope retains completed checkpoints, optional end, failure boundary,
        primary diagnostic, ordered cleanup diagnostics, cancellation, accepted/drained sequence
        facts, and full Cause. Its constructor/member spelling beyond settled forms is not
        invented.

        ### Exact condition-to-code mapping

        | Invalid condition | Diagnostic code |
        | --- | --- |
        | Invalid descriptor ID | InvalidDescriptorId |
        | Duplicate ID, operation ID, or slot ID | DuplicateDescriptorId |
        | Duplicate machine value | DuplicateMachineValue |
        | Duplicate module ownership | DuplicateModuleOwnership |
        | Missing module reference | MissingModuleReference |
        | Invalid state default | InvalidStateDefault |
        | Unresolved operation requirement | UnresolvedRequirementId |
        | Invalid Story metadata | InvalidStoryMetadata |
        | Malformed trace/operation facts | InvalidTraceRecord |
        | Impossible lifecycle tuple | InvalidLifecycleTransition |
        | Inconsistent end/failure/cleanup | InvalidStoryEvidence |
        | AppPlan fingerprint mismatch | ArtifactIdentityMismatch |
        | Share kind supplied to raw import | WrongArtifactKind, before member/identity checks |
        | Invalid redaction operand/path | InvalidArtifactOperand |

        - Keep exact category, path, and bound details. Never return undefined or generic
        corrupt-artifact.

        - Reject deleted Scenario/v1/root/child/final/replay shapes, callbacks, Effects, fixtures,
        live refs, Runtime ownership, invalid discriminants, duplicates, bad defaults, and
        unresolved requirements.

    O5  Defensive byte export and redacted shares
        - importTraceArtifact accepts Uint8Array and returns an inferred private validated
        artifact. Caller mutation of input/output bytes cannot mutate the artifact or Runtime.

        - The exact accepted exportTraceArtifact(trace, options?) signature,
        ArtifactExportOptions, ArtifactCanonicalPath, ArtifactValuePath, and share union follow
        below. Omitted/empty redactions return byte-identical raw output.

        - A non-empty finite valid policy copies a validated raw trace to kind
        trace-share-artifact, version flow-state/trace-share-artifact.v2, changing only selected
        application-owned values and the required non-empty canonical redactions manifest.

        - Replace each exact existing caller tuple with the fixed [REDACTED] string. Natural
        unmanifested occurrences of that string are legal.

        - Never target identities, fingerprints, versions, discriminants, ordering, sequence,
        truncation, diagnostic codes, cleanup, or status structure. Reject missing, duplicate,
        overlapping, wildcard, structural, or string-form paths as InvalidArtifactOperand before
        projection.

        - Array indices are exact non-negative safe in-range integers. Canonical path order is
        segment-wise: numbers before strings, numeric order, UTF-8 string byte order without
        normalization, and shorter equal-prefix paths first.

        - Display paths use dotted identifiers and JSON-quoted brackets otherwise; displays are
        never parsed as input. All shared structural bounds include the manifest. Share output
        is always uncompressed.

        - No share importer/decoder, CLI consumption, sanitization, summary, diff, proof,
        compression, replay, persistence, boot, evidence authority, or mutation path exists.

        - Raw import checks kind first and rejects share as WrongArtifactKind before
        member/identity/fingerprint checks. Runtime, persistence, raw bytes, decoded data, and
        canonical evidence remain unchanged.

        - @V5 proves exact redaction bytes, privacy boundaries, rejection order, and defensive
        copies.

        - These fragments are normative type notation. StableId, TraceRecord, Checkpoint,
        StoryFailure, and Diagnostic name package-private schema components. They do not create
        public model exports. Their private nested representation remains pending Spec design at 
        @V4.

        - The target type fragments remain uncompiled and unexecuted.

        ```ts
        type ArtifactCanonicalPath = (string | number)[];
        type ArtifactValuePath =
          | ["records", number, "snapshot", "memory" | "context", ...ArtifactCanonicalPath]
          | [
              "records", number, "facts", number,
              "data" | "value" | "error" | "defect" | "latest", ...ArtifactCanonicalPath,
            ]
          | [
              "checkpoints", number, "actors", number, "snapshot",
              "memory" | "context", ...ArtifactCanonicalPath,
            ]
          | ["checkpoints", number, "snapshot", "memory" | "context", ...ArtifactCanonicalPath];
        ```

        ```ts
        type ArtifactExportOptions = {
          redactions?: { path: ArtifactValuePath; replacement: "[REDACTED]" }[];
        };
        ```

        ```ts
        declare function exportTraceArtifact(
          trace: ReturnType<typeof importTraceArtifact>,
          options?: ArtifactExportOptions,
        ): Uint8Array;
        ```

        ```ts
        type TraceShareArtifactBase = {
          kind: "trace-share-artifact";
          version: "flow-state/trace-share-artifact.v2";
          storyId: StableId;
          appId: StableId;
          persistenceVersion: string;
          appPlanFingerprint: string;
          capturedAt: NonNegative;
          truncatedBeforeSequence: Nullable<NonNegative>;
          records: TraceRecord[];
          checkpoints: Checkpoint[];
          redactions: ArtifactValuePath[];
        };
        ```

        ```ts
        type TraceShareArtifact =
          | (TraceShareArtifactBase & {
              outcome: "completed";
              end: Checkpoint;
              failure: null;
              cleanup: { status: "complete" };
            })
          | (TraceShareArtifactBase & {
              outcome: "failed";
              end: Nullable<Checkpoint>;
              failure: StoryFailure;
              cleanup: { status: "complete" } | { status: "failed"; diagnostics: Diagnostic[] };
            });
        ```

    O6  Trace queries and artifact comparison
        - Behavior diff and live behavior check compare the same canonical behavior projection,
        optionally scoped by module. They yield equal/different, never trace-only incomplete.

        - Trace summary reports counts/timeline for retained-window evidence and includes the
        marker; its outcome is completed or incomplete. Trace proof requires complete evidence
        and rejects every non-null marker with the EvidenceUnavailable meaning and marker.

        - No incomplete proof succeeds. Exact selector forms are issues, timeline, actor:<id>, and
        correlation:<id>. Split at the first colon and preserve the remaining ID. Selectors are
        data, never executable code. Complete-artifact absence has the UnknownSelector meaning.
        Both project through the canonical Diagnostic mapping below.

        - Query refusals use classification Failure and code InvalidArtifactOperand.
        EvidenceUnavailable and UnknownSelector are exact strings in Diagnostic.details.reason,
        not DiagnosticCode members or another expected-error family. The mapping is:

        | Query failure meaning | Exact diagnostic path |
        | --- | --- |
        | EvidenceUnavailable | ["artifact", "truncatedBeforeSequence"] |
        | UnknownSelector | ["selector"] |

        - For incomplete proof, details are { reason:"EvidenceUnavailable",
        truncatedBeforeSequence:N, selector:exactSelector }. N is the non-null truncation marker.
        For missing selector in complete evidence, details are { reason:"UnknownSelector",
        selector:exactSelector, truncatedBeforeSequence:null }. Preserve the exact selector string.
        All fields stay within Diagnostic's existing scalar/null domain.

        - Under truncation, absence is not ordinary unknown. Trace diff is equal only when both
        artifacts are complete and every selected ordered section matches. Differing retained
        facts are different even under truncation.

        - Matching retained facts with either input truncated are incomplete. Incompatible
        App/AppPlan identity is failure, not difference.

        - Sections are event-sequence, transitions, state-changes, issues, resource-patches,
        resource-freshness, transaction-outcomes, stream-outcomes, and timer-behavior. Default
        diff scope includes the complete ordered section set.

        - @plan.CommandLine.O3 owns process projection and exit status, without redefining these
        meanings. @V6 proves selector, comparison, identity, and incomplete-evidence behavior.

decisions
    D1  Canonical structure is shared and private
        - Adapters own I/O, applications own opaque codecs, and CLI owns transport and process
        output. One package-private model feeds Story/CLI projections. No extra decoder/public
        schema exists.
    D2  Share output has no evidence authority
        - Explicit redaction reduces application values without changing raw truth. A distinct
        kind and version prevent share bytes from being interpreted as raw evidence or
        executable input.
    D3  Observable guarantees replace internal choreography
        - Atomic cuts, global ordering, accepted-prefix drains, nonblocking sinks, complete causal
        consumer reads, and pre-commit capacity refusal are required. Permit/reservation/queue/
        gate/attachment/drain algorithms and graph-wide simultaneous publication are not
        prescribed.

constraints
    C1  Keep schemas, routes, and carriers closed
        - Preserve every known required wire field, discriminator, correlation, and bound. Add no
        alternate decoder, migration envelope, public TurnRecord/v2 artifact hierarchy, raw Cause
        carrier, or artifact-specific bound. @V4 leaves private representation design replaceable.
        Unsettled public call forms remain at @Q2. Private nested wire representation and its
        verification remain at @V4; they do not create a product field decision or public type.

        - @plan.PackageContract.O1 owns inspect-route temporary deferral and restoration gates.
    C2  Keep observation separate from execution and durability
        - No actor retention setter, mutable TraceLog, implicit persistent history, fabricated
        trace, sink-owned actor work, share input, or snapshot-based replay is added.
    C3  Checks are planned evidence obligations
        - Every check below is PLANNED and has not established this proposal's runtime behavior.
        Existing owner tests may supply a baseline only after their vectors and behavior are
        checked.

verification
    V1  Pure projections, public type privacy, and live inspection
        - Establishes @O1. PLANNED: inspect inert definitions/snapshots and live committed facts.

        - Pass when pure planning starts no work and makes no actual completion claims, live
        records match production truth, one formatter owns each projection, and private
        v2/TurnRecord types remain absent from fresh packed public declarations.

        - Reject fabricated/mutable lookalikes. Planned checks: check:types,
        check:packed-consumers, and inspect projection tests under
        packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/inspect/test. Missing forms are blocked only by @Q2.
    V2  Shared codec boundary, all bounds, and host portability
        - Establishes @O2. Every check is PLANNED and unrun.

        ### Byte validity

        - Scenarios: ASCII, 2/3/4-byte UTF-8, malformed UTF-8, 256/257-byte identity names,
        lone surrogates, and composed/decomposed spellings.
        - Pass: accepted inputs produce canonical portable bytes. Lone surrogates reject before
        encoding. UTF-8 byte counts never use UTF-16 length or silently normalize spelling.

        ### Structure and exact bounds

        - Scenarios: malformed JSON, detectable duplicate keys, cyclic data, serialized negative
        zero, non-finite values, and exact depth/node/array/string/property-key/byte boundaries.
        - Pass: each invalid input rejects before committed mutation or work. Bound failures name
        the exact limit and path; other categories remain distinguishable. No raw parser exception,
        undefined result, Buffer, raw Cause, or generic corruption escapes.

        ### Compression

        - Scenarios: one gzip member, concatenated members, trailing bytes, unsupported compression,
        and a decompressor crossing its output-byte bound.
        - Pass: exactly one supported member is accepted. Crossing the bound stops decompression;
        other malformed inputs retain their decompression or bounded-input diagnostic ownership.

        ### Identity and readiness validation

        - Scenarios: invalid versions/identities and opaque application-codec failure. Record inert
        construct, ready execution, storage read/decode, service acquisition, and store 
        installation.
        - Pass: construction reads nothing. ready validates detached boot and opaque values before
        acquisition, installation, activation, or handle escape. Failure leaves committed state
        unchanged. Artifact-only paths invoke no boot-domain decoder or gateway.

        ### Portability and checks

        - Run full vectors on pinned Node. Run the same compiled codec's compact fixture on Bun,
        Deno, Chromium, Gecko, and WebKit; record exact host versions.
        - Pass: equal accepted input yields equal bytes/digests on every tested host.
        - Planned checks: codec tests under packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/artifact/tests and
        check:types. Structural limits, ordering, and host portability remain unproved.

    V3  Atomic evidence, sinks, truncation, and disposal races
        - Establishes @O3. Every check is PLANNED and unrun.

        ### Committed ordering and lifecycle

        - Scenarios: interleave actor/store publication, acknowledgment, StoreFanout, and lifecycle
        events. Read the existing actor handle from each lifecycle listener.
        - Pass: one global sequence contains committed cuts only. Acknowledgment follows evidence
        acceptance. StoreFanout stays causal. Listeners see the new lifecycle snapshot before its
        event; lifecycle emits no machine turn or terminal synthetic TurnRecord.

        ### Stalled and failing sinks

        - Scenarios: hold one sink callback indefinitely, then fail another while a healthy sink
        continues. Publish a later actor/store change and wait for its acknowledgment and fanout.
        - Pass: acknowledgment and StoreFanout complete without releasing the held callback.
        A failed attachment detaches alone; healthy sinks continue. No sink mutates or rolls back
        committed state, mixes Runtime state, or silently omits accepted evidence.

        ### Capacity, attachment, and truncation

        - Scenarios: zero/default/custom capacity, pre-attachment snapshots, late attachment,
        retained-tail clear, and accepted queued records arriving after clear.
        - Pass: pre-attachment is empty/null. Late attach marks greatest unseen sequence unless
        zero. Capacity and greatest-omitted markers are exact. Clear preserves sequence and marks
        only through the retained tail; queued accepted records may still arrive afterward.

        ### Drain, exhaustion, and disposal

        - Scenarios: accepted-prefix drain/dispose, sequence exhaustion before commit, skipped
        changed-ref revisions, and reverse-dependency Runtime disposal with terminal evidence.
        - Pass: drain/dispose waits through only its accepted prefix. Exhaustion refuses before
        mutation and retains cleanup credits. Skipped readers reread state. Disposal drains accepted
        terminal lifecycle evidence. Only explicit export persists records; buffers are not durable.

        - Existing candidate baseline:
        packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/evidence-sink-owner.test.ts.
        Its existence is not proof of this contract. Required vectors remain planned.

    V4  Canonical v2 schema and diagnostic ownership
        - Establishes @O4.

        - PLANNED: behavior/raw trace round-trip, UTF-8 canonical ordering, authored order, null
        fields, SHA-256 recomputation, receiving AppPlan mismatch, every operation union,
        requirement resolution, lifecycle tuple, end/failure/cleanup combination, and negative
        mapping listed at @O4.

        - Pass only with exact decoded-model/byte parity, no private schema export, and specific
        diagnostics before Runtime/application acquisition. Planned checks: artifact schema
        tests under packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/artifact/tests, check:types, and packed
        consumers.

        - Standalone scenarios: import/render valid behavior and trace bytes with no App or
        gateway. Alter behavior preimage/fingerprint coherence; separately compare incompatible
        artifact operands and supply a mismatched App at an existing context-bearing boundary.
        - Pass: behavior recomputation rejects altered bytes. Standalone trace validation needs
        no unavailable App. Actual receiving-App or operand mismatches reject as
        ArtifactIdentityMismatch. Byte import gains no argument or hidden authority.

        - Private nested representation and executable schema/fingerprint vectors remain pending.
        No complete undeclared v2 grammar or passed proof is claimed.

        - Pending Spec design: define one closed private representation for each nested layout
        below. Preserve all explicit public fields, round-trip laws, identity/closure/order,
        unknown-member rejection, diagnostics, compatibility exclusions, and structural bounds.
        These are representation choices within the fixed capability, not new public types.

        - Behavior requirements/modules/machines/Story metadata: identity, ownership,
        requirements and defaults.
        - TurnRecord and LifecycleRecord: committed cut, provenance, sequence and 
        lifecycle/revisions.
        - Checkpoint actor/runtime entries: exact membership, snapshot, now and pendingWork.
        - StoryFailure, cleanup/cancellation/sequence facts: primary failure and ordered cleanup.
        - Resource/transaction/stream wire facts: closed status and field-correlation laws.
        - Context/store/timer/issue wire facts: exact identities, revisions and order.
        - CliResult/CliError nested data/envelope: command/outcome vocabulary and process truth.


        - The named fields above do not establish an otherwise absent complete v2 grammar.
        Validate the designed representation through one production decoder and exact positive/
        negative schema vectors. Do not infer behavioral requirements from implementation.

    V5  Raw bytes, deterministic share export, and protected-field rejection
        - Establishes @O5.

        - PLANNED: omitted/empty policies, every tuple lane, natural replacement strings,
        numeric/string/prefix ordering, multibyte names, overlapping/duplicate/missing/
        wildcard/structural/out-of-range paths, manifest bounds, malformed share input, and
        caller mutations.

        - Pass with byte-identical default raw output, deterministic uncompressed share, unchanged
        protected fields/raw/Runtime/persistence, and WrongArtifactKind before raw schema or
        identity checks. All share consumers reject.

        - Planned production decoder/encoder tests route the redaction proof obligation; they
        remain unimplemented and unrun.
    V6  Trace queries, ordered comparisons, and incomplete evidence
        - Establishes @O6. PLANNED: complete equal/different, differing retained truncated,
        matching truncated, absent actor/correlation, malformed selector, first-colon IDs,
        module slicing, all ordered section selections, and incompatible app/fingerprint.

        - Pass with exact outcomes, marker-bearing EvidenceUnavailable meaning, complete-only
        proof/equality, and no gateway/Runtime execution. Pair these tests with
        @plan.CommandLine.V3 process vectors. Required tests remain planned and unimplemented.

        - Drive incomplete-proof and complete-missing-selector failures through
        programmatic and installed CLI paths. Decode each emitted diagnostic through Diagnostic.
        Assert Failure/InvalidArtifactOperand, the exact reason/path/marker/selector at @O6,
        stderr-only output, empty stdout, and exit 2. Separate query and code-set tests cannot 
        substitute for this check.

questions
    Q2  Which final call forms cover the remaining activities and artifact scenarios?
        These candidates require approval. They add no settled API or public support alias.

        - Activities: inspectActivities(machine, snapshot) returns an inferred projection for the
        exact Machine. It describes current declarations and inputs derivable from that snapshot.
        It starts no work. It invents no previous selection, retained P, generation or actual start.
        History-dependent onMemory factories cannot be reconstructed from this cut alone.
        No retained public declaration fixes this function's exact arguments or result.

        - Behavior: buildBehaviorContract({ app, stories: storiesRecord }) returns an inferred
        private behavior contract. buildBehaviorContract({ app }) permits declaration-only analysis.
        renderBehaviorCoverage({ app, stories: storiesRecord }, { moduleId: "orders" }) returns
        string.
        Its stories field and second options argument are optional; options has moduleId?:string.
        The one target requires app and uses the accepted external-ID Story record when supplied.
        Both functions remain inert. The branded non-empty gateway stays CLI-only under
        @plan.StoriesAndModels.O6. Retained declarations fix one target object and optional legacy
        Story array, but do not fix the replacement record's final typing or validation.

        - Compression: compressTraceArtifact(importedTrace): Promise<Uint8Array> and
        decompressTraceArtifact(bytes: Uint8Array): Promise<inferred imported trace>. Both use the
        shared bounded raw-trace codec and one supported gzip member. Reject shares; ordinary raw
        export remains uncompressed. Proposed terminal JavaScript rejection carries canonical
        Diagnostic. Earlier declarations returned undefined on failure and a v1 decoded carrier.
        Exact final success types and asynchronous terminal failure channels remain unapproved.

        - Trace analysis: `analyzeTrace` remains a required route name. Its exact parameters and
        return shape are unapproved; @plan.Artifacts.C4 leaves its form unspecified.

        - Behavior slicing: `sliceBehaviorContract` remains a required route name. Its exact
        parameters and return shape are unapproved; @plan.Artifacts.C4 leaves its form unspecified.

        - Behavior diff rendering: `renderBehaviorDiff` remains a required route name. Its exact
        parameters and return shape are unapproved; @plan.Artifacts.C4 leaves its form unspecified.
